#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:316
#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:754
msgid "Sizes made only on demand"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:432
msgid "What WordPress does with a file as it arrives. Both of these apply to new uploads and to anything you rebuild from here; images already on the site keep what they were given until then."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: name of the image library in use, e.g. Imagick.
#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:437
#, php-format
msgid "This server resizes with %s."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: the quality WordPress uses by default.
#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:454
#, php-format
msgid "1 to 100, used for every JPEG and PNG copy WordPress writes. WordPress uses %s when nothing says otherwise; higher means larger files."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:466
msgid "pixels"
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: the threshold WordPress uses by default.
#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:471
#, php-format
msgid "An upload longer than this on its longest edge is scaled down to it, and the untouched original is kept beside it. WordPress uses %s. Set 0 to keep every upload at the size it arrived."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:494
msgid "Bring the untouched original down to this limit as well"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:497
msgid "The original is what the sub sizes are cut from; once they are cut it only sits there, and on a photograph from a modern camera it is most of what the library weighs. With this on it is scaled to the limit like everything else - on upload, and for images already here the next time they are rebuilt."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:500
msgid "This cannot be undone: the full resolution the original was uploaded at is gone, and no size larger than the limit can be cut from it afterwards."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:482
msgid "Optimising re-encodes a file that is already the right size, to get the same picture in fewer bytes. Nothing is resized and no file is renamed, the result is kept only when it is genuinely smaller, and the untouched original beside a scaled upload is never touched."
msgstr ""

#. translators: 1: opening link tag to the Rebuild tab, 2: closing link tag.
#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:489
#, php-format
msgid "For the images already on the site, %1$srun it over the library%2$s, or optimise single images from the media library. Turning this on covers new uploads and anything you rebuild."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:518
msgid "This server does not let PHP run other programs, so the image optimisers cannot be used at all. Optimising falls back to writing the image out again, which gains little on files WordPress has already written."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:525
msgid "Optimising uses the programs installed on the server. They re-pack a file rather than re-encode it, which is where the real saving is; without them all this can do is write the image out again, and WordPress wrote it at this quality already."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:532
msgid "installed"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:532
msgid "missing"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:542
msgid "None of them is installed, so optimising will save almost nothing. Ask the host to install jpegoptim and optipng - they are in every distribution and need no configuration."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:554
msgid "Allow pngquant, which drops a PNG to fewer colours"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:556
msgid "Off by default because it changes the picture, not only the file. On screenshots, logos and flat graphics it typically halves the size with nothing visible to show for it; on photographs saved as PNG, look before you keep it."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:565
msgid "Also optimise the full size original kept beside a scaled upload"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:568
msgid "That file is usually the largest on disk and nothing on the site ever serves it: WordPress keeps it so a size can be cut again later. It is treated as the archive copy it is - only the lossless programs touch it, never a re-encode, never a lossy pass, and it is never sent to a service."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:578
msgid "None - use what is on this server"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:585
msgid "API key"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:590
msgid "A service does the same job as the programs above, usually better, and it is the answer on a host that has none and will not let PHP run one. Every file is sent to the service and every file counts against the monthly allowance - a photograph with nine sizes is nine files."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:593
msgid "TinyPNG compresses one way only, which is lossy, so it is used only when lossy compression is allowed above. ShortPixel follows that setting. The untouched original is never sent either way."
msgstr ""

#. translators: 1: number of files, 2: how long ago.
#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:601
#, php-format
msgid "The service last reported %1$s files used this month, %2$s ago."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: what the service said.
#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:616
#, php-format
msgid "The last time a file was sent, the service said: %s"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:631
msgid "Optimise images as they are uploaded and after a rebuild"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:650
msgid "Do it in the background, after the upload has finished"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:653
msgid "Optimising a file, and writing its WebP and AVIF copies, both happen while the browser is still waiting for the upload. With this on the upload finishes as soon as the sizes are written and the rest is queued, which is what makes uploading a batch of photographs feel quick. An image is served in its own format for the minute or two before the queue reaches it."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:658
msgid "The queue runs on Action Scheduler, which this site has."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:660
msgid "The queue runs on WP-Cron, which needs a visit to the site to move: on a quiet site it works through the queue slowly. Action Scheduler is used instead wherever a plugin provides it."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %s: number of images.
#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:667
#, php-format
msgid "%s image is waiting in the queue."
msgid_plural "%s images are waiting in the queue."
msgstr[0] ""
msgstr[1] ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:680
msgid "1 to 100, used when re-encoding. Below the compression setting above there is something to gain; at or above it, most files will already be as small as they get."
msgstr ""

#. translators: 1: opening link tag, 2: closing link tag.
#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:690
#, php-format
msgid "Image URLs on the front end are rewritten to %1$swsrv.nl%2$s, a free image proxy that resizes and re-encodes on the fly and serves the result from a CDN. Your own files are untouched, and turning this off puts every URL back."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:697
msgid "The proxy fetches each image from your site, so it only works on a site that is reachable from the internet. On a local or password protected site the images will not load."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:707
msgid "Rewrite front end image URLs to the proxy"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:722
msgid "WebP files are a good deal smaller than JPEG or PNG and every current browser reads them."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:729
msgid "WordPress normally cuts every registered size the moment a file is uploaded, most of which no page ever asks for. With this on, an upload keeps only its own file and each size is cut the first time something requests it, then stays on disk like any other."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:732
msgid "Uploads get quicker and the uploads folder stays smaller. The first visitor to need a size waits for it to be cut, and until then an image has no other size to offer in its srcset."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:742
msgid "Cut each size when it is first requested, not on upload"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:744
msgid "Rebuilding from the Rebuild tab still generates whatever you select there."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:756
msgid "This site has no image sizes registered."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:767
msgid "A size ticked here is never written on upload and is skipped by a rebuild, whatever the Rebuild tab has selected. It is cut the first time a page asks for it. Useful for the big sizes only a handful of pages ever use."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:775
msgid "A WebP copy is written next to every image WordPress generates, and the front end serves it through a picture element with the original as the fallback. Your JPEG and PNG files stay where they are, and a copy that would be larger than the original is thrown away."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:778
msgid "Every browser still in use reads WebP, so this is the safe copy to make. AVIF, below, is smaller again and is offered ahead of it where both are on."
msgstr ""

#. translators: 1: opening link tag to the Rebuild tab, 2: closing link tag.
#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:784
#, php-format
msgid "New uploads are converted as they arrive. For the images already in the library, %1$srun a rebuild%2$s once after turning this on."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:792
msgid "The image library on this server cannot write WebP, so no copies will be made. Ask your host for GD or Imagick with WebP support."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:797
msgid "This works on its own, but the proxy above is on and already delivers WebP from its CDN for the images it rewrites. The copies made here are what gets served whenever the proxy is off or does not handle an image."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:808
msgid "Convert generated images to WebP and serve the copies"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:817
msgid "1 to 100, used when writing the WebP copies."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:824
msgid "The same thing again in AVIF, which is a good deal smaller than WebP at the same quality. Where both are on, the picture element offers AVIF first and the browser falls back to the WebP copy, and then to the original, when it cannot read it."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:827
msgid "Encoding AVIF costs several times the processing that WebP does, so uploads and rebuilds take noticeably longer with this on. On a small server it is worth turning on for new uploads and rebuilding the library in batches."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:831
msgid "The image library on this server cannot write AVIF, so no copies will be made. It needs WordPress 6.5 or later with GD or Imagick built against an AVIF encoder - ask your host. Nothing breaks meanwhile: the WebP copies go on being served."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:842
msgid "Convert generated images to AVIF and serve the copies"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:851
msgid "1 to 100, used when writing the AVIF copies. The scale is not the same one JPEG and WebP use - AVIF around 60 looks about like JPEG at 82, and going higher costs a lot of file size for very little."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-atr-settings.php:859
msgid "1 to 100. Around 80 is the usual compromise between file size and artefacts."
msgstr ""

#: assets/js/admin.js:53
msgid "This server has no image optimiser installed - jpegoptim, optipng and the like - so all this can do is write the image out again, which wins nothing on files WordPress has already written. The settings say which are missing."
msgstr ""

#: assets/js/admin.js:57
msgid "These PNG files are already as small as lossless optimising makes them. Allowing lossy PNG in the settings lets pngquant take them further."
msgstr ""

#: assets/js/admin.js:61
msgid "These are PNG files, which do not get smaller by re-encoding. AVIF and WebP copies are what helps for those."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %d: the quality setting, e.g. 82.
#: assets/js/admin.js:66
#, js-format
msgid "They were already optimised at quality %d."
msgstr ""

#. translators: %d: the quality setting, e.g. 82.
#: assets/js/admin.js:70
#, js-format
msgid "The files are already as small as quality %d makes them; lower it in the settings to gain more."
msgstr ""

